1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,249
A kite tugs on its string.

2
00:00:02,249 --> 00:00:04,190
A swing hangs from two ropes.

3
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:06,536
A team leans back in a tug of war.

4
00:00:06,936 --> 00:00:08,850
Each time, a rope is pulling.

5
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:12,138
And that pull can change,
even when the load does not.

6
00:00:12,588 --> 00:00:16,382
At her grandmother's village, Diya
pulls a bucket of water up from the

7
00:00:16,382 --> 00:00:17,282
well.

8
00:00:17,218 --> 00:00:18,784
Holding it still is easy.

9
00:00:18,784 --> 00:00:22,596
But when she starts to pull it up
faster, the rope feels heavier.

10
00:00:22,596 --> 00:00:24,162
The water did not change.

11
00:00:24,662 --> 00:00:26,854
So what is the rope really doing?

12
00:00:26,854 --> 00:00:28,356
The answer is tension.

13
00:00:29,006 --> 00:00:30,297
So what is tension?

14
00:00:30,647 --> 00:00:34,497
When a rope is pulled tight, it
pulls on whatever is tied to it.

15
00:00:34,497 --> 00:00:36,348
That pull is the tension, T.

16
00:00:36,748 --> 00:00:40,219
Tension always points along
the rope, away from the body.

17
00:00:40,219 --> 00:00:42,814
A rope can pull, but it can never push.

18
00:00:43,214 --> 00:00:47,179
For a light rope, with almost no mass,
the tension is the same all along

19
00:00:47,179 --> 00:00:48,079
it.

20
00:00:47,963 --> 00:00:50,222
A smooth pulley only turns the rope.

21
00:00:50,222 --> 00:00:53,486
So Diya's pull is the same
as the pull on the bucket.

22
00:00:54,136 --> 00:00:55,683
To the motion notebook.

23
00:00:55,683 --> 00:00:58,305
A five kilogram bucket hangs on the rope.

24
00:00:58,655 --> 00:01:01,366
Its weight, m g, is forty nine newtons.

25
00:01:01,366 --> 00:01:04,942
Hanging still, the rope pulls
up with exactly forty nine.

26
00:01:05,342 --> 00:01:07,865
Now Diya pulls it up faster and faster.

27
00:01:07,865 --> 00:01:11,432
The acceleration is two metres
per second squared, upward.

28
00:01:11,832 --> 00:01:12,973
Slow-mo replay.

29
00:01:12,973 --> 00:01:15,159
Freeze, and draw the forces.

30
00:01:15,509 --> 00:01:17,371
T is now fifty nine newtons.

31
00:01:17,371 --> 00:01:21,715
It beats the weight by ten, and those
ten newtons speed the bucket up.

32
00:01:22,115 --> 00:01:23,075
Let it run.

33
00:01:23,075 --> 00:01:25,250
Now it rises at a steady speed.

34
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:28,188
a is zero, so T falls back to forty nine.

35
00:01:28,588 --> 00:01:30,871
Near the top, she lets it slow down.

36
00:01:30,871 --> 00:01:33,467
a points down, and T drops to thirty nine.

37
00:01:33,867 --> 00:01:36,888
On the graph, T moves
along a straight line.

38
00:01:36,888 --> 00:01:40,095
Each unit of upward
acceleration adds five newtons.

39
00:01:40,745 --> 00:01:41,658
Your turn.

40
00:01:41,658 --> 00:01:45,174
The bucket rises at a
steady one metre per second.

41
00:01:45,174 --> 00:01:47,671
Is T bigger than m g, or equal?

42
00:01:47,671 --> 00:01:49,014
Pause and decide.

43
00:01:52,214 --> 00:01:53,114
Equal!

44
00:01:52,882 --> 00:01:55,402
Steady speed means no acceleration.

45
00:01:55,402 --> 00:01:58,568
So the forces balance, and T equals m g.

46
00:01:59,218 --> 00:02:01,890
Now let's write it down,
one step at a time.

47
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:03,140
Step one.

48
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:05,295
Two forces act on the bucket.

49
00:02:05,295 --> 00:02:07,819
T pulls up, and m g pulls down.

50
00:02:08,569 --> 00:02:09,469
Step two.

51
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:11,078
Take up as positive.

52
00:02:11,078 --> 00:02:13,446
The net force is T minus m g.

53
00:02:13,446 --> 00:02:16,248
By the second law, that equals m a.

54
00:02:16,998 --> 00:02:18,121
Step three.

55
00:02:18,121 --> 00:02:19,696
Move m g across.

56
00:02:19,696 --> 00:02:21,858
T equals m g plus m a.

57
00:02:21,858 --> 00:02:25,081
That is, T equals m times g plus a.

58
00:02:25,831 --> 00:02:26,753
Step four.

59
00:02:26,753 --> 00:02:30,793
Hanging still, or moving at
a steady speed, a is zero.

60
00:02:30,793 --> 00:02:32,224
So T equals m g.

61
00:02:32,974 --> 00:02:33,957
Step five.

62
00:02:33,957 --> 00:02:36,635
If a points down, a is negative.

63
00:02:36,635 --> 00:02:39,267
Then T equals m times g minus a.

64
00:02:40,017 --> 00:02:41,087
Remember this.

65
00:02:41,087 --> 00:02:45,076
Tension follows the acceleration,
not the direction of motion.

66
00:02:45,776 --> 00:02:46,879
Side by side.

67
00:02:46,879 --> 00:02:50,719
On the left, the bucket is
pulled up faster and faster.

68
00:02:50,719 --> 00:02:53,691
a points up, so T is m times g plus a.

69
00:02:53,691 --> 00:02:55,100
Fifty nine newtons.

70
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:58,683
On the right, it is
lowered faster and faster.

71
00:02:58,683 --> 00:03:01,737
a points down, so T is m times g minus a.

72
00:03:01,737 --> 00:03:02,705
Thirty nine.

73
00:03:03,305 --> 00:03:04,368
Now you try.

74
00:03:04,368 --> 00:03:07,549
The bucket is still
rising, but slowing down.

75
00:03:07,549 --> 00:03:09,696
Is T more or less than m g?

76
00:03:09,696 --> 00:03:11,018
Pause and decide.

77
00:03:14,218 --> 00:03:15,118
Less!

78
00:03:14,796 --> 00:03:18,256
It is slowing while moving
up, so a points down.

79
00:03:18,256 --> 00:03:20,456
Then T is m times g minus a.

80
00:03:21,106 --> 00:03:22,841
Now, one worked example.

81
00:03:23,191 --> 00:03:27,606
The five kilogram bucket is pulled
up at two metres per second squared,

82
00:03:27,606 --> 00:03:29,124
over a smooth pulley.

83
00:03:29,124 --> 00:03:30,848
How hard must Diya pull?

84
00:03:30,848 --> 00:03:32,474
g is nine point eight.

85
00:03:32,974 --> 00:03:36,382
a points up, so T equals m times g plus a.

86
00:03:37,132 --> 00:03:40,236
That is five times nine
point eight plus two.

87
00:03:40,236 --> 00:03:42,329
Five times eleven point eight.

88
00:03:42,329 --> 00:03:43,738
Fifty nine newtons.

89
00:03:44,488 --> 00:03:48,676
The rope is light and the pulley is
smooth, so the tension is the same

90
00:03:48,676 --> 00:03:49,576
all along.

91
00:03:49,477 --> 00:03:51,649
Diya pulls with fifty nine newtons.

92
00:03:52,399 --> 00:03:54,728
Ten newtons more than the bucket's weight.

93
00:03:54,728 --> 00:03:57,237
That is why starting
the pull feels heavier.

94
00:03:57,887 --> 00:03:59,353
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:03:59,703 --> 00:04:04,195
Many students say: the bucket moves
up, so T must be bigger than m g.

96
00:04:04,595 --> 00:04:06,923
But only the acceleration counts.

97
00:04:06,923 --> 00:04:09,493
At a steady speed, T equals m g.

98
00:04:09,493 --> 00:04:12,498
Rising and slowing, T is less than m g.

99
00:04:13,148 --> 00:04:14,545
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:14,545 --> 00:04:18,631
In free-body numericals: bodies
hanging on strings, and ropes that can

101
00:04:18,631 --> 00:04:19,531
break.

102
00:04:19,803 --> 00:04:21,008
Exam level now.

103
00:04:21,008 --> 00:04:23,839
A rope breaks above three hundred newtons.

104
00:04:23,839 --> 00:04:26,792
It is used to lower a forty kilogram sack.

105
00:04:26,792 --> 00:04:27,692
g is ten.

106
00:04:27,691 --> 00:04:30,256
What acceleration keeps the rope safe?

107
00:04:30,256 --> 00:04:31,513
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:36,913 --> 00:04:38,153
The answer is A.

109
00:04:38,153 --> 00:04:41,951
At least two point five metres
per second squared, downward.

110
00:04:42,451 --> 00:04:45,825
The weight is four hundred
newtons, more than three hundred.

111
00:04:45,825 --> 00:04:48,115
So the rope cannot hold the sack still.

112
00:04:48,615 --> 00:04:51,720
Going down, T equals m times g minus a.

113
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:55,667
Forty times ten minus a must
be at most three hundred.

114
00:04:56,167 --> 00:04:59,024
So ten minus a is at
most seven point five.

115
00:04:59,024 --> 00:05:01,671
a is at least two point five, downward.

116
00:05:02,271 --> 00:05:03,727
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:03,727 --> 00:05:05,224
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:08,424 --> 00:05:10,355
Can you answer these without help?

119
00:05:10,755 --> 00:05:11,655
One.

120
00:05:11,217 --> 00:05:14,626
Why does the rope feel heavier
when Diya starts the pull?

121
00:05:15,526 --> 00:05:16,426
Two.

122
00:05:16,011 --> 00:05:19,301
Why is the tension the same
all along a light rope?

123
00:05:20,201 --> 00:05:21,101
Three.

124
00:05:20,875 --> 00:05:23,437
A bucket rises at a steady speed.

125
00:05:23,437 --> 00:05:25,271
Is T bigger than m g?

126
00:05:26,171 --> 00:05:31,110
Next time: a heavy steel trunk is hard
to start, but easier to keep sliding.

127
00:05:31,110 --> 00:05:32,010
Why?

128
00:05:31,599 --> 00:05:34,361
We find out in static
and kinetic friction.
